            "title": "LESSONS LEARNED",
            "eyebrow": "CIT 220 • Unit 02.1",
            "subtitle": "Continuous Monitoring, Analytics & Implementation",
            "lead": "LESSONS LEARNED Common Pitfalls & Challenges !",
            "isTitleSlide": false,
            "isSectionDivider": false,
            "rawHtml": "<h3>LESSONS LEARNED</h3><p class=\"lead-statement\">Common Pitfalls &amp; Challenges !</p><div class=\"info-card mt-3\"><p>Treating it as one product Zero Trust is an architecture and a process, not a single tool you buy and switch on. ! Ignoring legacy systems Old applications that can&#x27;t support modern auth become silent gaps in the model. ! Over-restricting users Excessive friction drives risky workarounds like shadow IT and shared logins. ! Skipping the culture shift Without leadership buy-in and training, teams quietly route around new controls. EXAMPLE SCENARIO A manufacturer rolls out strict device-posture checks company-wide overnight, blocking access for the many plant-floor terminals still running legacy Windows 7 software that can&#x27;t be updated. Frustrated staff start emailing production data to personal accounts to keep working -- the very shadow-IT risk Zero Trust was meant to prevent. The fix: a phased exception process with compensating controls for legacy systems, not a blanket cutover. 18 / 20</p></div>",
            "cleanText": "LESSONS LEARNED Common Pitfalls & Challenges ! Treating it as one product Zero Trust is an architecture and a process, not a single tool you buy and switch on. ! Ignoring legacy systems Old applications that can't support modern auth become silent gaps in the model. ! Over-restricting users Excessive friction drives risky workarounds like shadow IT and shared logins. ! Skipping the culture shift Without leadership buy-in and training, teams quietly route around new controls. EXAMPLE SCENARIO A manufacturer rolls out strict device-posture checks company-wide overnight, blocking access for the many plant-floor terminals still running legacy Windows 7 software that can't be updated. Frustrated staff start emailing production data to personal accounts to keep working -- the very shadow-IT risk Zero Trust was meant to prevent. The fix: a phased exception process with compensating controls for legacy systems, not a blanket cutover. 18 / 20",
